===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|seller}}{{lb|en|archaic}} A [[person#Noun|person]] who [[sell#Verb|sells]] ([[woman#Noun|women]]'s) [[apparel]], [[accessory#Noun|accessories]], and other [[decorative#Adjective|decorative]] [[good#Noun|goods]], especially those [[originally]] [[manufacture#Verb|manufactured]] in [[Milan]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Winter's Tale|act=IV|scene=iv|page=293|column=1|passage=He hath ſongs for man, or vvoman, of all ſizes: No '''Milliner''' can ſo fit his cuſtomers vvith Gloues: {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Beaumont Fletcher Comedies and Tragedies|title=The Fair Maid of the Inn|act=II|scene=i|page=35|column=1|passage=''Hoſt''. Here comes my vvife and daughter. / {{...}} / ''Clovv''[''n'']. She is a pretty lure to dravv cuſtome to your ordinary. / ''Hoſt''. Doſt think I keep her to that purpoſe? / ''Clovv''. VVhen a Dove-houſe is empty, there is cumin-ſeed uſed to purloine from the reſt of the neighbours; {{...}} A '''Milliner''' has choice of Monkies, and Paraketoes; {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Richardson Pamela|volume=IV|letter=XLII|chapter=From Miss Darnford, in Answer to the Preceding|page=285|passage=[H]e vvill not vviſh to get out of that narrovv, that exceeding narrovv Circle; and, in my Opinion, ſhould keep no Company, but that of Tailors, VVigpuffers, and '''Milaners'''.}}
# {{senseid|en|hatmaker}}{{lb|en|specifically}} A person [[involve]]d in the [[design#Noun|design]], [[manufacture#Noun|manufacture]], or [[sale]] of [[hat#Noun|hats]] for women.
#: {{hypernyms|en|hatmaker|hatter}}
#* {{RQ:Guardian 1713|issue=149|page=2|column=1|passage=The '''''Milliner''''' muſt be thoroughly verſed in Phyſiognomy; in the Choice of Ribbons ſhe muſt have a particular regard to the Complexion, and muſt ever be mindful to cut the Head-dreſs to the Dimentions of the Face.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=M[ary] J. Howell|chapter=Lesson II. On Lining Straw and Other Bonnets.|title=The Hand-book of Millinery;{{nb...|Comprised in a Series of Lessons for the Formation of Bonnets, Capotes, Turbans, Caps, Bows, etc.: To which is Appended a Treatise on Taste, and the Blending of Colours; also an Essay on Corset Making.}}|location=London|publisher=[[w:Simpkin & Marshall|Simpkin, Marshall, & Co.]],{{nb...|Stationers’ Hall Court, Ludgate Hill; and may be had of the authoress, 310, Regent Street, & 2, Lamb’s Conduit Place, Foundling.}}|year=1847|page=24|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.97341/page/n40/mode/1up|oclc=5005455|passage=The great difficulty generally experienced by amateur '''milliners''' in lining bonnets, is mainly attributable to the error of fixing the lining in the first instance to the edge of the bonnet, instead of arranging it previously at the head part.}}
#* {{RQ:Macaulay History of England|volume=I|chapter=III|page=346|passage='''Milliners''', toymen, and jewellers came down from London [to Tunbridge Wells], and opened a bazaar under the trees.}}
#* {{RQ:Dickens David Copperfield|page=235|passage=She is at present apprenticed, Miss Mowcher, or articled, or whatever it may be, to Omer and Joram, Haberdashers, '''Milliners''', and so forth, in this town.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=[[w:William Henry Perkin|Perkins]]’s Purple|editor=w:Charles Dickens|journal=[[w:All the Year Round|All the Year Round. A Weekly Journal. [...] With which is Incorporated Household Words]]|location=London|publisher={{...|Published at the Office, No. 26, Wellington Street, Strand. Printed by}} C. Whiting,{{nb...|Beaufort House, Strand.}}|date=10 September 1859|volume=I|issue=20|page=470|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/allyearround01dick/page/470/mode/1up|column=1|oclc=781591950|passage=We may, therefore, fairly suppose that the first '''milliner''' was probably contemporaneous with the first woman, and that the carpenters who made the ark were not ignorant of the construction of a bandbox.}}
#* {{RQ:Lewis Babbitt|chapter=XIX|section=III|page=243|passage=They went to a musical comedy and nudged each other at the matrimonial jokes and the prohibition jokes; they paraded the lobby, arm in arm, between acts, and in the glee of his first release from the shame which dissevers fathers and sons Ted chuckled, "Dad, did you ever hear the one about the three '''milliners''' and the judge?"}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Alison Matthews-David|title=Fashion Victims: The Damages of Dress Past and Present|location=London|publisher=w:Bloomsbury Publishing|year=2015|isbn=978-1-84520-449-5|page=92|passage=In the 1880s, '''milliners''' decorated hats with entire stuffed birds.}}

