===Verb===
{{en-verb}} {{term-label|en|transitive|archaic}}

# To [[manufacture#Verb|manufacture]] ([[woman#Noun|women]]'s [[apparel]], specifically [[hat#Noun|hats]]); also, to [[supply#Verb|supply]] (someone) with women's apparel, specifically hats.
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=Steuart’s Sallust, and The Eclectic Review|journal=[[w:Anti-Jacobin Review|The Anti-Jacobin Review and Magazine, or, Monthly Political and Literary Censor]]|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed by}} B. M‘Millan,{{nb...|Bow Street, Covent Garden.}}|month=December|year=1806|volume=XXV|issue=CII|page=429|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/sim_the-antijacobin-review-and-protestant-advocate_1806-12_25/page/429/mode/1up|oclc=1003934408|passage=We pass over his ridiculous observation {{...}} that {{w|Sallust}} has been "man-'''millinered''' by Dr. [Henry] Steuart;" for on what we do not understand we can make no remarks.|footer={{small|A figurative use.}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Edward John Trelawny|chapter=XLVI|title=Adventures of a Younger Son.{{nb...|[...] In Three Volumes.}}|location=London|publisher={{w|Henry Colburn}} and [[w:Richard Bentley (publisher)|Richard Bentley]],{{nb...|New Burlington Street.}}|year=1831|volume=III|page=321|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=zyL4DmoeX1gC&pg=PA321|oclc=11937065|passage=In the east, the only "study of mankind, is man." They have no Miss [[w:Maria Edgeworth|[Maria] Edgeworth]], nor any of those '''millinering''' cutters-out of human nature into certain patterns of given rules in education.|footer={{small|An adjectival use.}}}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=Compton Hall; or, The Recollections of Mr. Benjamin Walker. Chapter XI. A Clue and Its Issue.|journal=[[w:The Rambler (Catholic periodical)|The Rambler. A Catholic Journal and Review]]|location=London|publisher=Burns and Lambert,{{nb...|17 Portman Street, and 63 Paternoster Row. Sold by all booksellers.}}|month=May|year=1855|volume=III (New Series), part XVII|pages=353–354|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/a634760403londuoft/page/354/mode/1up|oclc=847979670|passage=Oh, if I had but a decent little income, enough to make her tolerably comfortable! For you know she couldn't go on '''millinering''' if she was married to me. My mother wouldn't stand that.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Obadiah [pseudonym]|title=A Morning at a Fashionable Church|journal=[[w:Harper's Weekly|Harper’s Weekly. A Journal of Civilization]]|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Harper (publisher)|Harper & Brothers]]|date=16 January 1858|volume=II|issue=55|page=33|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/sim_harpers-magazine_1858-01-16_2_55/mode/1up|column=4|oclc=883997577|passage=Their eyes were very busy—a '''millinering''' I should say. The lady in front of us had her book upside down; the two behind us got into a violent quarrel about somebody's bonnet, which one of the two said was new, while the other pretended it was an old one turned.|footer={{small|A figurative use.}}}}
#* {{RQ:Shaw Cashel Byron's Profession|chapter=III|page=34|passage=You will find that my dressmaker, Madame Smith, is to be depended on for work, though she is expensive and dishonest. When we are tired of Wiltstoken, we will go to Paris, and be '''millinered''' there; but in the meantime we can resort to Madame Smith.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Rex Beach|chapter=The Chronicle of a Chromatic Bear Hunt|title=Oh, Shoot! Confessions of an Agitated Sportsman|location=New York, N.Y.&#59; London|publisher=[[w:Harper (publisher)|Harper & Brothers]]|year=1921|page=94|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/ohshootconfessio00beaciala/page/94/mode/1up|oclc=1745530|passage=Out on the lake we began to feel more fully the immensity and the desolation of the place. {{...}} Floating all about us were bergs from the size of a water goblet to the size of the ''Lusitania'', {{...}} We traced features of men and shapes of beasts in them. Some wore preposterous hats, '''millinered''' by the sun itself.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Henry Bingham|chapter=105|title=The Sons of Adam|location=Garden City, N.Y.|publisher=GuildAmerica Books|year=2003–2004|page=360|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/sonsofadam0000bing_o4c1/page/360/mode/1up|isbn=978-1-58288-136-2|passage=Tom's hat should have bitten the bullet and surrendered then and there. The battering it took in the next half-minute was indescribable. Tom twisted and wrung it between his fingers. It had arrived at the house a brand-new hat. It would leave a cheaply '''millinered''' corpse.|footer={{small|An adjective use.}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Kevin Brown (historian)|Kevin Brown]]|chapter=Business Not as Usual|title=Fighting Fit: Health, Medicine and War in the Twentieth Century|location=Stroud, Gloucestershire|publisher=w:The History Press|year=2008|page=66|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/fightingfithealt0000kevi/page/66/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-7509-4649-0|passage=The crowds of besuited men and '''millinered''' women going about their affairs ostensibly as in peacetime and watching the scene cannot remain unaffected by the consequences of what they see.|footer={{small|An adjective use.}}}}
# {{lb|en|figurative}} To [[adorn#Verb|adorn]] or [[decorate]] (something).
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=|authorlink=|title=Reviews and Literary Notices. ''The Book of the Sonnet.'' Edited by {{w|Leigh Hunt}} and S[amuel] Adams Lee. Boston: Roberts Brothers.|journal=[[w:The Atlantic|The Atlantic Monthly. A Magazine of Literature, Art, and Politics]]|location=Boston, Mass.|publisher={{w|Ticknor and Fields}},{{nb...|124 Tremont Street}}|month=April|year=1867|volume=XIX|issue=CXIV|page=510|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/atlanticmonthlym8268dall/page/510/mode/1up|column=2|oclc=612185692|passage=We would not have Poesy to be greatly '''millinered''', whatever fashions other ladies may adopt; and when we meet her corseted in the iron framework of the sonnet's rhymes, and crinolined about with the unyielding drapery of its fourteen lines, we feel that she is no doubt elegantly dressed, but we long to see her in any other attire she is wont to put on.}}

